{
  "generated_text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the properties of the angles in a trapezoid and the given condition that the angles form an arithmetic sequence.\n\n1. **Sum of Angles in a Trapezoid:**\n   The sum of the interior angles of any quadrilateral, including a trapezoid, is \\(360^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Arithmetic Sequence:**\n   Let the four angles of the trapezoid be \\(a\\), \\(a+d\\), \\(a+2d\\), and \\(a+3d\\), where \\(a\\) is the smallest angle and \\(d\\) is the common difference of the arithmetic sequence.\n\n3. **Given Information:**\n   We know that the smallest angle \\(a = 60^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Sum of the Angles:**\n   The sum of the angles in the trapezoid can be written as:\n   \\[\n   a + (a+d) + (a+2d) + (a+3d) = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(a = 60^\\circ\\):\n   \\[\n   60^\\circ + (60^\\circ + d) + (60^\\circ + 2d) + (60^\\circ + 3d) = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the Equation:**\n   Combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ + d + 2d + 3d = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   240^\\circ + 6d = 360^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n6. **Solve for \\(d\\):**\n   Isolate \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   6d = 360^\\circ - 240^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6d = 120^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = 20^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n7. **Find the Largest Angle:**\n   The largest angle is \\(a + 3d\\):\n   \\[\n   a + 3d = 60^\\circ + 3 \\times 20^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + 3d = 60^\\circ + 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + 3d = 120^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nThus, the measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120^\\circ}\\).",
